export const MAIN_HTML = "<article class=\"hub\">\n\n<header class=\"hub-head\">\n<h1>FAA FRAT: What the Flight Risk Assessment Tool Is and How to Use One</h1>\n<p class=\"byline\">PlaneWX \u00b7 Updated October 6, 2026</p>\n\n<p class=\"answer\">An FAA FRAT is a Flight Risk Assessment Tool: a form for recording the hazards of a planned flight and the risk they add up to, before you commit to it. The FAA publishes FRAT guidance, sample forms, and an online FAASTeam FRAT so pilots can turn the <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-pave\" data-gl=\"pave\">PAVE</a> checklist into a written check. \"The form used to record each hazard is known as a Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT).\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span> It does not make the decision: \"The Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT) is not designed to make the go/no-go decision for you\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>. Official FAA FRAT guidance: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/general/flight-risk-assessment-tool-frat-faa-safety-team\">FAA Safety Team FRAT page</a>.</p>\n\n<section class=\"takeaways\" aria-label=\"Key takeaways\">\n<h2>Key takeaways</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>FAA FRAT means Flight Risk Assessment Tool. The FAA publishes the guidance and sample tools; the pilot in command still decides&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>A FRAT turns the PAVE checklist into a written, repeatable check, so the risk picture does not live only in your head&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-15]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Green is not a green light. The FAA Safety Team says even a high green score should prompt a closer look&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>A FRAT only works if the inputs are honest. The FAA warns that pilots skew them toward a lower score when they want to go&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 4-2]</a></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</header>\n\n<aside class=\"keyfacts\" aria-label=\"Key facts about flight risk assessment tools\">\n<details class=\"kf-box\" id=\"key-facts\" open>\n<summary><h2>Key facts</h2></summary>\n<ol>\n  <li>FAA FRAT means Flight Risk Assessment Tool&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>A <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-frat\" data-gl=\"frat\">FRAT</a> is the form for recording each hazard of a flight and its risk&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Most FRATs are organized by <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-pave\" data-gl=\"pave\">PAVE</a>: Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-11]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Numerical FRATs add up a score that falls in three ranges, usually green, yellow, and red&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>A FRAT does not make the go/no-go decision for you&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Even a high green score deserves a second look&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>The FAA's sample FRAT dates to InFO 07015, published in 2007&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>The FAA Safety Team offers an online FRAT&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>If the flight changes, redo the FRAT on a fresh form&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 5-4]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>The pilot in command is the final authority on the flight&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6, para. (a)]</a></span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"kf-foot\">Hover, focus, or tap any dotted term for a definition and its official source.</p>\n<div class=\"kf-fade\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>\n</details>\n</aside>\n\n<div class=\"hub-body\">\n<p>Every flight carries some risk. The FAA's original FRAT guidance puts the job this way: \"Every flight has hazards and some level of risk associated with it.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span> A FRAT makes you name those hazards before you are in the airplane, when changing the plan is still cheap. This page explains what an FAA FRAT is, where the FAA FRAT comes from, how its scores work, how to fill one out honestly, and how PlaneWX's electronic FRAT (eFRAT) with WX Score differs from a paper form. It draws on the FAA Risk Management Handbook, the FAA Safety Team, FAA InFO 07015, and AC&nbsp;120&#8209;92D&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4]</a></span>. For the product announcement, see the <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/news/frat\">PlaneWX FRAT news item</a>. Related risk guides: <a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/weather-risk\">weather risk and personal minimums</a> and <a href=\"/learn/risk-stacking\">risk stacking</a>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"what-is-a-frat\">What is a flight risk assessment tool?</h2>\n<p>The Risk Management Handbook defines it by what it holds: \"Identifying hazards and associating them with risks can be accomplished using a simple spreadsheet or form during flight planning. The form used to record each hazard is known as a Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT).\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span></p>\n<p>Most FRATs are built on <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-pave\" data-gl=\"pave\">PAVE</a>, the FAA's hazard checklist. The acronym \"represents hazards that relate to the pilot, the aircraft, the environment, and external pressures.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 1-2]</a></span> The two work together: \"Pilots can use the PAVE checklist before flight to identify hazards. Pilots can use a FRAT to organize identified hazards and associated risks for additional analysis.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-15]</a></span> The Pilot section usually includes <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-imsafe\" data-gl=\"imsafe\">IMSAFE</a>, the fitness check for illness, medication, stress, alcohol, fatigue, and emotion&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-5]</a></span>. For the full checklist, see the <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/pave-risk-assessment-weather\">PAVE guide</a>.</p>\n<p>A weather briefing is not a FRAT. The briefing feeds one box, the environment. The FRAT asks about everything else too: your sleep, the airplane's squawks, the runway, and the people waiting at the other end.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"form-checklist-tool\">FRAT form, checklist, and tool: what those searches mean</h2>\n<p>Search phrases like \"FRAT form,\" \"FRAT checklist,\" and \"FRAT tool\" usually point at the same FAA idea, seen from three angles:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>FRAT form.</strong> The written record itself. \"The form used to record each hazard is known as a Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT).\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span> Paper, spreadsheet, or app all count.</li>\n  <li><strong>FRAT checklist.</strong> Often means the PAVE checklist that finds the hazards a FRAT then records&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-15]</a></span>. PAVE is the checklist; the FRAT is the form.</li>\n  <li><strong>FRAT tool.</strong> The working copy you fill out for a specific flight, including the <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/general/flight-risk-assessment-tool-frat-faa-safety-team\">FAA Safety Team</a> online FRAT and any operator or app version adapted from FAA samples&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>None of those labels changes the job: name the hazards, score or narrate the risk, mitigate what you can, and leave the Fly or Stay call with the PIC&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"origins\">Where does the FAA FRAT come from?</h2>\n<p>The FAA's FRAT material is spread across a few documents, and only some of them describe the tool in detail. Other core FAA references, including the PHAK, the AIM, and the Aviation Weather Handbook, do not use the term. AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92 covers PAVE and personal minimums but does not define a FRAT. These documents do:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"stack\" role=\"table\">\n<thead role=\"rowgroup\"><tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">FAA document</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Date</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">What it says about FRATs</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody role=\"rowgroup\">\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/other_visit/aviation_industry/airline_operators/airline_safety/inFO07015.pdf\">InFO 07015, Flight Risk Assessment Tool</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Date\">July 3, 2007</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What it says about FRATs\">Introduces a sample FRAT built by the GAJSC Turbine Aircraft Operations Subgroup, for operators to adapt with their own score thresholds&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/risk-management-handbook-faa-h-8083-2a\">Risk Management Handbook, FAA&#8209;H&#8209;8083&#8209;2A</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Date\">2022</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What it says about FRATs\">The main teaching text: numerical and narrative FRATs, the risk matrix, and a worked case study&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, chs. 3 to 5]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/general/flight-risk-assessment-tool-frat-faa-safety-team\">FAA Safety Team FRAT page</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Date\">Updated October 28, 2024</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What it says about FRATs\">How to read green, yellow, and red, with a link to the FAASTeam online FRAT&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_120-92D_FAA_Web.pdf\">AC&nbsp;120&#8209;92D, Safety Management Systems</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Date\">May 21, 2024</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What it says about FRATs\">Mentions FRATs only in passing, to separate a per-flight FRAT from an organization's <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-sms\" data-gl=\"sms\">SMS</a> risk assessment&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4, para. 3.4.5.4.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/blog/clearedfortakeoff/silver-linings-silver-bullets-and-other-fictions-avoiding-vfr-flight\">FAA Safety Briefing, VFR into IMC article</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Date\">Updated July 1, 2026</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What it says about FRATs\">Recommends a FRAT to enforce personal minimums&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The 2007 InFO introduced the tool for operators: \"The Turbine Aircraft Operations Subgroup as part of the General Aviation Joint Steering Committee has developed a risk assessment tool for use in flight operations.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span> It references the original AC&nbsp;120&#8209;92, since replaced; the current version is AC&nbsp;120&#8209;92D, which draws the line between tools: \"A FRAT would be used to determine if conditions are acceptable for a specific flight to commence.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4, para. 3.4.5.4.1]</a></span></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"scoring\">How does FRAT scoring work?</h2>\n<h3>Numerical FRATs</h3>\n<p>\"A numerical FRAT lists different hazards and an associated number, which indicates the significance of each hazard. The pilot selects the relevant hazards, and derives a total score. Typically, the score falls into three ranges.\" Low scores indicate low risk, the middle range says exercise caution, and scores above the upper threshold indicate danger&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span>. They are quick, but the Handbook lists four drawbacks&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-11]</a></span>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\"The form may not cover all the hazards for a particular flight.\"</li>\n  <li>\"The range of scores for each risk level may appear arbitrary, leading a pilot to question the results.\"</li>\n  <li>\"The form does not encourage the pilot to analyze each hazard.\"</li>\n  <li>\"The form may indicate an overall low level of risk, leading the pilot to ignore the implications of a particular hazard.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Narrative FRATs and the risk matrix</h3>\n<p>\"This type of FRAT asks the user to identify hazards and associate each hazard with risk.\" It takes longer but addresses many of the drawbacks&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-11]</a></span>. For each hazard you judge two things, because \"Risk is the composite of the likelihood (probability) and the severity (consequences) of a particular outcome.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 4-1]</a></span> A risk matrix combines them into low, medium, serious, or high. The Handbook's reading of the colors: a medium result implies go, but \"risk should still be mitigated if possible.\" Serious or high imply no-go unless you can reduce the risk and reassess&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 4-2]</a></span>.</p>\n<h3>Reading green, yellow, and red</h3>\n<p>The FAASTeam FRAT, \"like many other FRATs, produces a score that will fall within one of three risk categories: Green (low), yellow (medium), and red (high).\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span> Its guidance for each:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"stack\" role=\"table\">\n<thead role=\"rowgroup\"><tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Score</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">FAA Safety Team guidance</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">What to do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody role=\"rowgroup\">\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Green (low)</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"FAA Safety Team guidance\">Not an automatic go. \"Even a score on the higher end of the green range should prompt further examination.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span></td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to do\">Find the items that drove the score and reduce them where you can</td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Yellow (medium)</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"FAA Safety Team guidance\">Reduce the high-scoring items, for example by waiting for better weather or taking a more familiar airplane&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span></td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to do\">If it stays yellow, get a flight instructor or FAASTeam Representative to look at it with you&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Red (high)</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"FAA Safety Team guidance\">\"Should your score land in the red zone, it's crucial to think about cancelling the flight unless the associated risks can be safely managed.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span></td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to do\">Change the flight until the risk drops, or stay on the ground</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The FAA also warns against letting a deadline override red. Its example is a son's graduation, and its advice is that it is \"always preferable to wish you were flying rather than wishing you were on the ground.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"honest\">How do you use a FRAT honestly?</h2>\n<p>A FRAT is only as good as what you put in it. The Risk Management Handbook names the problem directly: \"Pilots sometimes use inputs that skew the risk toward a lower level due to the desire to complete a flight.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 4-2]</a></span> A few habits keep it honest:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>When unsure, rate it higher.</strong> \"In such cases, pilots should apply the more conservative parameter(s), which place the risk at a higher level.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 4-2]</a></span></li>\n  <li><strong>Fill it out close to departure.</strong> \"The matrix results lose relevance if the hazards change before a flight departs.\" If the plan changes, such as a new day or a different airplane, redo it on a fresh form&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, pp. 4-2 and 5-4]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Set thresholds that can say no.</strong> If your FRAT has never come out yellow, check the scale. The FAA's warning about thresholds that never trigger applies to one pilot as much as to a fleet&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Look at the worst item, not just the total.</strong> One high hazard can hide in a low total&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-11]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Name the pressure.</strong> \"The pressure to get to a particular destination or to leave at the conclusion of a planned stay can affect a pilot\u2019s judgment.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-8]</a></span> Write it in the External section, even when it feels minor. Work the weather early so that pressure does not lock the plan; see <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/route-weather-planning-before-pressure-builds\">route weather planning before the pressure builds</a>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ask someone.</strong> The FAA suggests an instructor or FAASTeam Representative when a score stays yellow&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>, and the Handbook adds: \"If there is any doubt concerning the outcome of a flight, the pilot should consider hiring an instructor or a mentor pilot and making the trip a learning experience.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 4-6]</a></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Many experienced pilots run PAVE in their heads, and that discipline deserves respect. The cost is that it all lives in working memory, often under schedule pressure. The FAA Safety Team's view: \"Simply running through the task mentally often overlooks the actual risk exposure.\" \"The mind has a tendency to isolate individual risks, which can lead to an underestimation of their collective impact.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span> Writing it down makes the process repeatable. The judgment stays yours.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"safety\">Why do FRATs matter for GA safety?</h2>\n<p>Because the hard accidents are rarely about one hazard. The FAA's analysis of NTSB data on <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-vfr\" data-gl=\"vfr\">VFR</a> flight into instrument conditions found that \"An analysis of the National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) accident database from 2014 to 2023 reveals 180 IMC-related general aviation accidents, with a fatality rate exceeding 82%.\" It also found that \"more than 40% of accidents show some level of intentional flight into IMC.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span> The FAA's recommended intervention: \"At the operational level, using a flight risk assessment tool (FRAT) is a good way to enforce standards, such as personal minimums.\" \"FRATs are an excellent way to establish self-accountability and detect building risk before it becomes apparent in any one factor.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span> A FRAT pairs naturally with <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/personal-minimums-weather-planning\">written personal minimums</a> and with watching how small items stack; see <a href=\"/learn/risk-stacking\">risk stacking</a>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"first-frat\">How do you do your first FRAT?</h2>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Pick a form.</strong> The FAASTeam online FRAT, the Handbook's PAVE form, or an app&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-11]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Get the weather first.</strong> A complete briefing is the input for the environment section.</li>\n  <li><strong>Go letter by letter.</strong> Pilot (IMSAFE and currency), Aircraft (squawks, fuel, performance, runway), enVironment (weather, night, terrain, airports), External (who is waiting and why)&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, pp. 3-4 to 3-11]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mitigate the top items.</strong> Use <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-team\" data-gl=\"team\">TEAM</a>: transfer, eliminate, accept, or mitigate each risk, then rescore&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. G-4]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Decide, and keep the record.</strong> Go, delay, change the plan, or stay. After the flight, compare what you rated with what you found. \"A pilot\u2019s risk assessment accuracy should improve with practice.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 4-5]</a></span></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"paper-vs-planewx\">How does a paper FRAT compare with PlaneWX's eFRAT?</h2>\n<p>Both follow PAVE, and both leave the decision to you. Electronic FRATs are not new: the FAASTeam FRAT has an online version, and the FAA notes that \"FRATs can be as simple as a basic spreadsheet.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span> What PlaneWX changed is where the weather part starts. Mark Wolfgang, PlaneWX's founder, built the WX Score to put a number on weather risk against your own minimums, and PlaneWX pioneered an eFRAT that integrates that score, so the weather rating begins from your briefing instead of a blank line.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"stack\" role=\"table\">\n<thead role=\"rowgroup\"><tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Question</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Paper or spreadsheet FRAT</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">PlaneWX eFRAT</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody role=\"rowgroup\">\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Where the weather rating starts</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Paper or spreadsheet FRAT\">A blank line you fill from your briefing</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"PlaneWX eFRAT\">A hint from your WX Score breakdown, which you can rate differently with a short note&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">When you fill it out</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Paper or spreadsheet FRAT\">Whenever you choose</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"PlaneWX eFRAT\">Opens within 4 hours of departure, so it reflects how you are today&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Who sets the rating</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Paper or spreadsheet FRAT\">You</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"PlaneWX eFRAT\">You. PlaneWX pre-fills facts as hints; your rating overrides its suggestion&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Stacked risks</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Paper or spreadsheet FRAT\">Only if you add them up</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"PlaneWX eFRAT\">A Risks are stacking banner at three or more elevated factors&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">The record</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Paper or spreadsheet FRAT\">Wherever you file it</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"PlaneWX eFRAT\">Saved with the flight and included in the Final Pre-Flight Briefing email&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Where to get it</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Paper or spreadsheet FRAT\">The FAASTeam online FRAT is linked from the FAA Safety Team page&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span></td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"PlaneWX eFRAT\">Included on every PlaneWX plan, including Free&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The PlaneWX column comes from the help center&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span>. Pick whichever form you will actually use every time.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"field-reference\">Where are the official FRAT references?</h2>\n<section class=\"fieldref\" aria-labelledby=\"fieldref-h\">\n<h3 id=\"fieldref-h\">Official FRAT references</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Start here:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf#page=28\">Risk Management Handbook, Chapter 3, Using a Flight Risk Assessment Tool (PDF pages 28 to 29)</a>, with the risk matrix in <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf#page=34\">Chapter 4 (PDF pages 34 to 39)</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/general/flight-risk-assessment-tool-frat-faa-safety-team\">FAA Safety Team FRAT page</a> and the <a href=\"https://explore.dot.gov/#/site/FAA/views/FRATTOOL/FRATTool?:iid=1\">FAASTeam online FRAT</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/other_visit/aviation_industry/airline_operators/airline_safety/inFO07015.pdf\">InFO 07015, Flight Risk Assessment Tool (2007)</a>, with the original sample form&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_120-92D_FAA_Web.pdf#page=49\">AC&nbsp;120&#8209;92D, paragraph 3.4.5.4.1 (PDF page 49)</a>, for FRATs and SMS&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4]</a></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"status\">Links checked October 6, 2026: all returned HTTP 200 or 206 (partial PDF response), except the online FRAT, a dashboard that blocks automated checks; it opens in a browser from the FAA Safety Team page.</p>\n</section>\n\n<h2 id=\"decision\">Where does a FRAT fit in a disciplined decision?</h2>\n<p>PlaneWX is decision support for general aviation pilots, built around one idea: \"Fly like it's your job.\" Professional crews run the same process on every trip, and a FRAT is the part of that process most pilots skip. The loop is <span class=\"loop\">Brief, then FRAT, then Fly or Stay, then Debrief</span>, with a Mentor if you want one. Here is how PlaneWX runs each beat, as described in the help center&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Brief.</strong> The WX Score rates the weather against your minimums as Favorable (75% and up), Marginal (50 to 74%), or Unfavorable (under 50%). \"A low score doesn't mean don't fly. It means don't fly without asking why the number is what it is.\" (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/wx-score\">WX Score</a>)</li>\n  <li><strong>FRAT.</strong> \"PlaneWX FRAT is a dynamic flight risk assessment, not a static one-time form.\" It opens within 4 hours of departure, because \"The 4-hour window keeps the self-assessment honest and timely.\" Known facts such as recent hours and night currency are pre-filled as hints, and \"Your rating overrides the PlaneWX suggestion.\" Rating the weather more optimistically than the WX Score hint requires a short note (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/frat\">FRAT</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Fly or Stay.</strong> You record GO, NO-GO, Postpone, or Modify. \"PlaneWX never recommends GO or NO-GO. You make the call as PIC.\" \"The courage to stay is part of the loop, not a failure of it.\" (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/frat\">FRAT</a>, <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a>)</li>\n  <li><strong>Debrief.</strong> Self Debrief compares the FRAT picture with how the flight went. It is a PlaneWX Labs feature for Pro Plus and does not change the WX Score (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Mentor.</strong> Need Help Now unlocks within 2 hours of departure and asks for a FRAT inside the 4-hour window, so a mentor sees more than the weather (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/mentors\">Mentors</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>FRAT is on every plan, including Free: \"We will never gate safety behind a subscription.\" (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/free-plan\">The Free Plan</a>) And the limits are stated plainly: \"PlaneWX is not a substitute for a complete, independent pre-flight weather briefing. Pull one from a source you already trust before you fly.\" (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a>) Density altitude is a common Aircraft and enVironment hazard; see the <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/learn/aviation-weather/density-altitude\">density altitude guide</a>.</p>\n\n<!-- Screenshot slots (dark mode; Chief of Staff review required before adding; real trips only, blur tail numbers and names):\n     S1: FRAT enVironment section with the WX Score breakdown hint and the weather self-rate.\n     S2: Risks are stacking banner.\n     S3: Decision step with GO / NO-GO / Postpone / Modify. -->\n\n<p><a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/\">Run your next FRAT in PlaneWX</a>, read <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/frat\">how the PlaneWX FRAT works</a>, or see the <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/news/frat\">FRAT launch news</a>.</p>\n\n<nav class=\"hub-related\" aria-labelledby=\"related-h\">\n<h2 id=\"related-h\">More from the PlaneWX Learning Center</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/weather-risk\">Weather Risk and Personal Minimums: FAA Definitions and Where to Go Next</a>. How the FAA defines hazard, risk, personal minimums, and PAVE, with links into the risk guides.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/risk-stacking\">Risk Stacking: How Small Risks Add Up to a Big One</a>. Why no single item has to be dangerous for the flight to be dangerous, and how a FRAT can show the stack.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/blog/pave-risk-assessment-weather\">PAVE Risk Assessment and Weather</a>. The Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment, and External pressures checklist that feeds a FRAT.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/blog/personal-minimums-weather-planning\">Personal Minimums for Weather Planning</a>. Writing limits you will honor when the FRAT and the briefing put pressure on the plan.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/blog/route-weather-planning-before-pressure-builds\">Route Weather Planning Before the Pressure Builds</a>. Work the weather early so External pressure does not lock the plan before you fill out a FRAT.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/taf\">What Is a TAF? How to Read a Terminal Aerodrome Forecast</a>. The airport forecast, with FM, TEMPO, PROB30, and a full worked decode.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/metar\">What Is a METAR? How to Read an Airport Weather Report</a>. The airport observation, every group decoded, plus when a SPECI is issued.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/airmet-sigmet\">AIRMET, SIGMET, and CWA: In-Flight Weather Advisories Explained</a>. G-AIRMETs, SIGMETs, Convective SIGMETs, and Center Weather Advisories, with decoded samples.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/pirep\">What Is a PIREP? How to Read and Give a Pilot Weather Report</a>. The UA and UUA format, the icing and turbulence scales, and how to give one.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/winds-aloft\">Winds and Temperatures Aloft: How to Read an FB Forecast</a>. The FB code decoded, including winds over 100 knots, and how model winds differ.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/icing\">Icing Forecasts: CIP, FIP, Freezing Level, and Icing Intensity</a>. What the icing products show, what their intensity words mean, and where they fall short.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/turbulence\">Turbulence Forecasts: GTG, GTG-N, and the Intensity Scale</a>. The automated turbulence forecast and nowcast, EDR, and the advisories that cover what GTG does not.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/weather-radar\">Weather Radar for Pilots: NEXRAD, TDWR, Reflectivity, and Datalink Delay</a>. How to read radar images, their blind spots, and why cockpit mosaics are older than they look.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/density-altitude\">Density Altitude Calculator and Guide: What It Is and How to Calculate It</a>. The FAA definition, the formulas, a worked example, a calculator, and the Koch chart.</li>\n</ul>\n</nav>\n\n<section class=\"faq\">\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n\n<h3>What is an FAA FRAT?</h3>\n<p>FAA FRAT means Flight Risk Assessment Tool: the FAA's name for a form that records the hazards of a planned flight and the risk they add up to. \"The form used to record each hazard is known as a Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT).\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span> The FAA publishes FRAT guidance on the <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/general/flight-risk-assessment-tool-frat-faa-safety-team\">FAA Safety Team FRAT page</a>, sample forms in the Risk Management Handbook and InFO 07015, and an online FAASTeam FRAT&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>What is a FRAT in aviation?</h3>\n<p>A flight risk assessment tool: a form, spreadsheet, or app for recording the hazards of a planned flight and the risk they add up to. \"The form used to record each hazard is known as a Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT).\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span> It is the pilot's chance to sit down and do an honest assessment, considering all factors in the PAVE checklist.</p>\n\n<h3>What does FRAT stand for?</h3>\n<p>Flight Risk Assessment Tool&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span>. In FAA and GA use, people also search for \"FAA FRAT,\" \"FRAT tool,\" \"FRAT form,\" and \"FRAT checklist\"; those phrases all point at the same idea.</p>\n\n<h3>What is a FRAT form?</h3>\n<p>The written record of the hazards and risk for one planned flight. The Risk Management Handbook defines the FRAT as that form: \"The form used to record each hazard is known as a Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT).\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-10]</a></span> It can be a paper sheet, a spreadsheet, or an app. The FAA's original sample form is attached to InFO 07015&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>What is a FRAT checklist?</h3>\n<p>Pilots often say \"FRAT checklist\" when they mean the PAVE checklist that feeds a FRAT. PAVE is how you find hazards (Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures). A FRAT is where you record them and assess the risk&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-15]</a></span>. The Pilot section usually includes IMSAFE&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-5]</a></span>. See the <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/pave-risk-assessment-weather\">PAVE guide</a>.</p>\n\n<h3>What is a FRAT tool?</h3>\n<p>Any form, spreadsheet, or app that records flight hazards and risk. The FAA Safety Team publishes guidance and links to an online FAASTeam FRAT&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>. The Risk Management Handbook also shows numerical and narrative sample tools&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, pp. 3-10 and 3-11]</a></span>. A FRAT tool assists planning; it does not make the go/no-go decision for you&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>Is a FRAT required by the FAA?</h3>\n<p>None of the FAA documents on this page makes a FRAT mandatory for personal Part 91 flying. The FAA Safety Team says a FRAT ought to be part of every flight's planning, and the Risk Management Handbook says a FRAT is appropriate for any flight but may not be necessary for less complex ones&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 5-4]</a></span>. Operators with a safety management system may use FRATs as part of it&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4, para. 3.4.5.4.1]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>Does a green FRAT mean I should fly?</h3>\n<p>No. \"The Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT) is not designed to make the go/no-go decision for you; it serves as a tool to assist in planning your flight and considering a broader spectrum of hazards and risks.\" Even a high green score should prompt a closer look&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>. The decision stays with the pilot in command&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>How does PlaneWX's electronic FRAT (eFRAT) use the WX Score?</h3>\n<p>PlaneWX's eFRAT is built on PAVE like the FAA forms. The WX Score rates the weather against your minimums as Favorable, Marginal, or Unfavorable and appears as a hint in the enVironment section. Your LOW / MEDIUM / HIGH self-rates drive the FRAT; rating weather more optimistically than the hint needs a short note. The eFRAT opens within 4 hours of departure, flags stacked risks, and never recommends GO or NO-GO. You make the Fly or Stay call as PIC&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>How is PlaneWX FRAT different from a paper FRAT?</h3>\n<p>It is an eFRAT built on PAVE like the FAA forms, but it opens within 4 hours of departure, pre-fills facts it already knows as hints, shows the WX Score breakdown as a weather hint, and flags stacked risks. Your ratings drive the result, and PlaneWX never recommends GO or NO-GO&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-7\">[7]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>Where can I get the FAA FRAT?</h3>\n<p>Start on the <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/general/flight-risk-assessment-tool-frat-faa-safety-team\">FAA Safety Team FRAT page</a>, which links to an online version of the FAASTeam FRAT&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>. The Risk Management Handbook also shows a sample numerical FRAT and a narrative PAVE form&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, pp. 3-10 and 3-11]</a></span>. InFO 07015 includes the original sample form for operators&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>What is the difference between PAVE and a FRAT?</h3>\n<p>PAVE is the checklist for finding hazards: pilot, aircraft, environment, and external pressures. A FRAT is where you record them and assess the risk&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, p. 3-15]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>How should I set FRAT score thresholds?</h3>\n<p>Realistically. The FAA's original FRAT guidance warns that \"If every flight is within the acceptable range under any condition, it is likely that the thresholds have not been set correctly.\"&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span></p>\n</section>\n\n<section class=\"glossary\" id=\"glossary\" aria-labelledby=\"glossary-h\">\n<h2 id=\"glossary-h\">Glossary</h2>\n<p>Every term below links to the FAA, NWS, or NOAA document that defines it. The same definitions appear when you hover, focus, or tap a dotted term on this page.</p>\n<dl>\n  <dt id=\"gl-frat\"><dfn>FRAT</dfn> (Flight Risk Assessment Tool)</dt>\n  <dd>A form or checklist for recording flight hazards and the risk they add up to before you fly. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf#page=28\">FAA Risk Management Handbook, Chapter 3, Using a Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT)</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-imsafe\"><dfn>IMSAFE</dfn> (Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol, Fatigue, Emotion)</dt>\n  <dd>A personal fitness checklist that helps a pilot spot aeromedical hazards, the P in PAVE, before a flight. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf#page=23\">FAA Risk Management Handbook, Chapter 3, Aeromedical</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-pave\"><dfn>PAVE</dfn> (Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures)</dt>\n  <dd>The FAA risk checklist that sorts preflight hazards into four groups: the pilot, the aircraft, the environment, and external pressures. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf#page=11\">FAA Risk Management Handbook, Chapter 1, The PAVE Checklist</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-sms\"><dfn>SMS</dfn> (Safety management system)</dt>\n  <dd>An organization-wide, preventive approach to safety with a safety policy, a positive safety culture, formal hazard and risk methods, and safety assurance. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_120-92D_FAA_Web.pdf#page=1\">FAA AC 120-92D, Chapter 1</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-team\"><dfn>TEAM</dfn> (Transfer, Eliminate, Accept, Mitigate)</dt>\n  <dd>The four ways to handle a risk once it is identified. Some FAA material orders it TEMA. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf#page=76\">FAA Risk Management Handbook, Glossary</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-vfr\"><dfn>VFR</dfn> (Visual flight rules): flight category</dt>\n  <dd>Ceiling greater than 3,000 feet and visibility greater than 5 miles. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html#7-1-7\">AIM, 7-1-7</a></span></dd>\n</dl>\n</section>\n\n<p class=\"note\">This page explains published FAA guidance. A FRAT is a planning aid, not a certification or an FAA requirement for personal flying, and no score makes the decision. The pilot in command is directly responsible for the flight&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6, para. (a)]</a></span>. Always get a current briefing before you fly.</p>\n\n<section class=\"refs\" aria-labelledby=\"references\">\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol>\n  <li id=\"ref-1\">FAA&#8209;H&#8209;8083&#8209;2A, \"Risk Management Handbook.\" Federal Aviation Administration. 2022. Pages cited: 1-2, 3-4 to 3-11, 3-15, 4-1, 4-2, 4-5, 4-6, 5-4, G-4. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/risk-management-handbook-faa-h-8083-2a\">https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/risk-management-handbook-faa-h-8083-2a</a>. PDF: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf\">https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-2\">\"Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT).\" FAA Safety Team, Federal Aviation Administration. Last updated October 28, 2024. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/general/flight-risk-assessment-tool-frat-faa-safety-team\">https://www.faa.gov/general/flight-risk-assessment-tool-frat-faa-safety-team</a>. Online FRAT: <a href=\"https://explore.dot.gov/#/site/FAA/views/FRATTOOL/FRATTool?:iid=1\">https://explore.dot.gov/#/site/FAA/views/FRATTOOL/FRATTool?:iid=1</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-3\">InFO 07015, \"Flight Risk Assessment Tool.\" Federal Aviation Administration, Flight Standards Service. July 3, 2007. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/other_visit/aviation_industry/airline_operators/airline_safety/inFO07015.pdf\">https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/other_visit/aviation_industry/airline_operators/airline_safety/inFO07015.pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-4\">AC&nbsp;120&#8209;92D, \"Safety Management Systems for Aviation Service Providers.\" Federal Aviation Administration. May 21, 2024. Paragraph 3.4.5.4.1 and Appendix G. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_120-92D_FAA_Web.pdf\">https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_120-92D_FAA_Web.pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-5\">\"Silver Linings, Silver Bullets, and Other Fictions: Avoiding VFR Flight Into Instrument Weather.\" FAA Safety Briefing, Federal Aviation Administration. Last updated July 1, 2026. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/blog/clearedfortakeoff/silver-linings-silver-bullets-and-other-fictions-avoiding-vfr-flight\">https://www.faa.gov/blog/clearedfortakeoff/silver-linings-silver-bullets-and-other-fictions-avoiding-vfr-flight</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-6\">14 CFR 91.3, \"Responsibility and authority of the pilot in command.\" Electronic Code of Federal Regulations. Accessed October 6, 2026. <a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91/subpart-A/section-91.3\">https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91/subpart-A/section-91.3</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-7\">PlaneWX Help Center: \"FRAT,\" \"The Risk Loop,\" \"WX Score,\" \"Mentors,\" \"The Free Plan.\" PlaneWX. Accessed October 6, 2026. <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/frat\">FRAT</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/wx-score\">WX Score</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/mentors\">Mentors</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/free-plan\">The Free Plan</a></li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</div>\n\n</article>"
export const INLINE_STYLE = "\n  :root {\n    --bg: #ffffff; --fg: #1b1f24; --muted: #57606a; --line: #d0d7de;\n    --card: #f6f8fa; --accent: #0b5cad; --quote: #eef4fb;\n  }\n  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\n    :root {\n      --bg: #0f1419; --fg: #e6e8eb; --muted: #9aa4af; --line: #2d3640;\n      --card: #161d24; --accent: #6cb4ff; --quote: #142231;\n    }\n  }\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  html { -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; }\n  body { margin: 0; background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg);\n    font: 18px/1.65 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; }\n  main { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 32px 20px 64px; }\n  h1 { font-size: 2.05rem; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 12px; }\n  h2 { font-size: 1.45rem; line-height: 1.3; margin: 44px 0 12px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }\n  h3 { font-size: 1.12rem; margin: 26px 0 8px; }\n  p, ul, ol { margin: 0 0 16px; }\n  li { margin: 0 0 6px; }\n  a { color: var(--accent); }\n  .byline { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin-bottom: 24px; }\n  .answer { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);\n    border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 18px; font-size: 1.06rem; margin: 0 0 20px; }\n  .takeaways { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px 4px; margin: 0 0 24px; }\n  .takeaways h2 { border: 0; margin: 0 0 8px; padding: 0; font-size: 1.05rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--muted); }\n  blockquote { margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 12px 16px; background: var(--quote); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 6px; }\n  blockquote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }\n  blockquote footer { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 6px; }\n  .cite { font-size: 0.78em; vertical-align: super; line-height: 0; text-decoration: none; white-space: nowrap; }\n  .cite:hover { text-decoration: underline; }\n  .cite + .cite { margin-left: 0.25em; }\n  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0 0 20px; font-size: 0.95rem; }\n  th, td { border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 10px 12px; vertical-align: top; text-align: left; }\n  th { background: var(--card); }\n  .checklist { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px 6px; margin: 0 0 20px; }\n  .checklist h3 { margin-top: 10px; }\n  .checklist ul { list-style: none; padding-left: 0; }\n  .checklist li::before { content: \"\\2610\"; margin-right: 8px; color: var(--muted); }\n  .faq h3 { margin-top: 22px; }\n  .note { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; font-style: italic; }\n  .refs ol { padding-left: 1.4em; font-size: 0.93rem; }\n  .refs li { margin-bottom: 10px; word-break: break-word; }\n  .refs li:target { background: var(--quote); outline: 2px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 4px; }\n  .loop { font-weight: 600; }\n  /* ---- Hub layout: header, Key Facts sidebar, body (PlaneWX /learn/aviation-weather template) ---- */\n  main.hub-main { max-width: 1120px; }\n  .hub { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0 40px; }\n  .hub-head, .keyfacts, .hub-body { min-width: 0; }\n  /* ---- Sub-headings: real h3, semibold, about 1.13x body, clear space above ---- */\n  .hub h3 { font-size: 1.13em; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.35; margin: 36px 0 10px; }\n  .hub .faq h3 { margin-top: 32px; }\n  .hub .fieldref h3 { margin-top: 4px; }\n  /* ---- Citations stay on the line of the word before them ---- */\n  /* A cite group never breaks inside, at any width (including Key Facts). Groups too long for the narrowest\n     container are split in the source so each cite sits right after its claim. */\n  .cites { white-space: nowrap; }\n  .cite + .cite { margin-left: 0; }  /* no gap before a cite that follows a cite */\n  /* ---- Key Facts ---- */\n  .kf-box { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-top: 4px solid var(--accent);\n    border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px 4px; margin: 0 0 24px; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.42; }\n  .kf-box summary { list-style: none; cursor: pointer; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }\n  .kf-box summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }\n  .kf-box summary::after { content: \"Show\"; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }\n  .kf-box[open] summary::after { content: \"Hide\"; }\n  .kf-box h2 { border: 0; margin: 0; padding: 0; font-size: 1rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--muted); }\n  .kf-box ol { margin: 8px 0 6px; padding-left: 1.25em; }\n  .kf-box li { margin: 0 0 6px; }\n  .kf-box .kf-foot { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.78rem; margin: 2px 0 8px; }\n  .kf-fade { display: none; }\n  /* ---- Official field reference box ---- */\n  .fieldref { background: var(--quote); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px 6px; margin: 0 0 22px; }\n  .fieldref .status { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }\n  /* ---- Raw code blocks (TAF, METAR, etc.) ---- */\n  .raw-wrap { position: relative; margin: 0 0 8px; }\n  pre.taf-raw { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px; overflow-x: auto; margin: 0;\n    font: 0.95rem/1.9 ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Liberation Mono\", monospace;\n    scrollbar-width: thin; scrollbar-color: var(--muted) var(--line); }\n  pre.taf-raw::-webkit-scrollbar { height: 8px; }\n  pre.taf-raw::-webkit-scrollbar-track { background: var(--line); border-radius: 0 0 8px 8px; }\n  pre.taf-raw::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background: var(--muted); border-radius: 4px; }\n  pre.taf-raw:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }\n  pre.taf-raw .tk { color: inherit; }\n  .scroll-note { display: none; }\n  .decode-cap { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.88rem; margin: 0 0 18px; }\n  .table-intro { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0 0 8px; text-align: left; }\n  /* ---- Tables ---- */\n  table.decode td:first-child { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Liberation Mono\", monospace; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }\n  table.decode tr:target td { background: var(--quote); }\n  table.times td, table.times th { white-space: nowrap; }\n  table.times th { white-space: normal; }\n  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }\n  .table-wrap table { margin: 0; }\n  code { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Liberation Mono\", monospace; font-size: 0.92em; }\n  /* ---- On-page glossary (no-JS fallback and tooltip anchor targets) ---- */\n  .glossary dl { margin: 0 0 16px; }\n  .glossary dt { font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600; margin-top: 14px; }\n  .glossary dfn { font-style: normal; }\n  .glossary dt:target, .glossary dt:target + dd { background: var(--quote); }\n  .glossary dd { margin: 2px 0 0; font-size: 0.95rem; }\n  .glossary .src { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }\n  /* ---- Below 600 px: stacked-card tables, scroll hint and fade on raw blocks ---- */\n  @media (max-width: 599.98px) {\n    table.stack, table.stack tbody, table.stack tr, table.stack td, table.stack th[scope=\"row\"] { display: block; width: 100%; }\n    table.stack thead { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap; }\n    table.stack tr { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; margin: 0 0 12px; overflow: hidden; }\n    table.stack td, table.stack th[scope=\"row\"] { border: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 8px 12px; }\n    table.stack tr > :first-child { border-top: 0; }\n    table.stack th[scope=\"row\"], table.decode td:first-child { background: var(--card); font-weight: 600; }\n    table.stack td[data-label]::before { content: attr(data-label); display: block; font: 600 0.74rem/1.4 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;\n      text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 2px; }\n    table.decode td:first-child::before { display: none; }\n    .table-wrap:has(table.stack) { overflow: visible; }\n    .scroll-note { display: block; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.82rem; margin: 0 0 6px; }\n    .raw-wrap::after { content: \"\"; position: absolute; top: 1px; right: 1px; bottom: 9px; width: 40px; pointer-events: none;\n      border-radius: 0 8px 0 0; background: linear-gradient(to right, rgba(0,0,0,0), var(--card)); transition: opacity .15s; }\n    .raw-wrap.at-end::after { opacity: 0; }\n    table.times { font-size: 0.88rem; }\n    table.times th, table.times td { padding: 8px 7px; }\n  }\n  /* ---- Desktop: two columns, sticky Key Facts (kept last so it overrides the mobile toggle styles) ---- */\n  @media (min-width: 980px) {\n    .hub { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 760px) 320px; justify-content: center; }\n    .hub-head { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }\n    .hub-body { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 2; }\n    .keyfacts { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1 / span 2; }\n    /* Capped only when taller than the viewport; a bottom fade shows while more facts are below. */\n    .keyfacts .kf-box { position: sticky; top: 16px; max-height: calc(100vh - 32px); overflow-y: auto; }\n    .keyfacts summary { pointer-events: none; }\n    .keyfacts .kf-box summary::after, .keyfacts .kf-box[open] summary::after { content: none; }\n    .kf-box.is-capped .kf-fade { display: block; position: sticky; bottom: -4px; height: 36px; margin: -36px -16px 0; pointer-events: none;\n      background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0,0,0,0), var(--card)); }\n    .kf-box.is-capped.at-end .kf-fade { opacity: 0; }\n  }\n  /* ---- Glossary tooltip (hover, keyboard focus, tap). No framework. ---- */\n  a.gl { color: inherit; text-decoration: underline dotted; text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 3px; cursor: help; }\n  a.gl:hover, a.gl[aria-expanded=\"true\"] { color: var(--accent); text-decoration-style: solid; }\n  a.gl:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; border-radius: 2px; }\n  .gl-tip { position: fixed; z-index: 1000; max-width: min(340px, calc(100vw - 24px)); background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg);\n    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 6px 24px rgba(0,0,0,.18); padding: 10px 12px;\n    font: 0.88rem/1.5 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; white-space: normal; text-align: left; }\n  .gl-tip[hidden] { display: none; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-t { display: block; font-weight: 700; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-x { display: block; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.82rem; margin-bottom: 4px; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-d { display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-s { display: block; font-size: 0.8rem; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-s a { color: var(--accent); }\n  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) { .gl-tip { transition: opacity .12s; } }\n"
